# Names the doctrine may cite that the tree does not define, each with
# its reason. A new entry here is a DECISION, not a convenience.
emit_loop: a NEGATIVE example — the law names it as a shape it refuses (a feature-named fn on a pass context)
get_owned: prose shortening of `avra_array_get_owned`, in a sentence that spells the full name beside it
filter_map: a construct on the sugar backlog, named as absent — the entry licenses a repetition until it lands
aligned_marks: a NEGATIVE example — the law names the window helper whose anchor list held one KIND of member and silently attached a mark to the wrong one; the anchor list that replaced it is `MarkWindows`
file_command: a HISTORICAL example — the "one definition" instance the law named for the CLI's shared registry; @std/cli as components replaced that registry
unified_lift: a HISTORICAL instance — style.doc_run_stolen names it as a fn that wore `accepts`'s doc; the generic seat law folded it into `agreed`, which judges a concrete seat by `accepts` alone
under_overlay: a HISTORICAL instance — compiler.bracket_ritual names it as the bracket fn taking a thunk that its matcher once found; the brackets are spelled at their sites now (lane C, S4b-2) and compiler.bracket_ritual is a native rule whose fixtures carry the shape
match_node: a HISTORICAL example — the node-variant law cites the matcher that trusted two equal shallows to share an arity; the structural matcher (`At`, `Shape`, `shape_at`) replaced it
tools/idioms.py: a HISTORICAL PATH — the regex idiom tool CLAUDE.md's ledgers cite by name for past incidents (a dead-alternative matcher, a missing ACCEPTED table); deleted once the language could state every idiom it ratcheted natively (avra-8sb5.25.16)
tools/idioms.sh: a HISTORICAL PATH — the shim that ran tools/idioms.py, deleted alongside it
tools/integrate.sh: a HISTORICAL PATH — the lane integrator CLAUDE.md's working discipline cites for a gate that outgrew a harness window; deleted once tools/land.sh became the one path that moves main
